1360 0 obj
<<
/Title (Racism, xenophobia, and religious intolerance are persistent global issues that undermine social cohesion, human rights, and the development of inclusive societies. These forms of discrimination stem from prejudice, fear of difference, and long-standing historical inequalities that continue to shape relations between communities. In an increasingly interconnected world, ensuring equal participation and protection for individuals of all ethnic, national, and religious backgrounds remains a major challenge.)
/Dest [25 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1348 0 R
/Next 1372 0 R
>>
endobj
1361 0 obj
<<
/Title (In the modern era, globalization and migration have increased diversity but also introduced new challenges. Rising nationalism, misinformation, online hate speech, and political polarization have intensified discrimination in many societies. Minority groups, migrants, refugees, and religious communities are often disproportionately affected, facing barriers to safety, opportunity, and inclusion.)
/Dest [25 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1348 0 R
/Prev 1373 0 R
>>
endobj
1362 0 obj
<<
/Title <>
/Dest [16 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1355 0 R
/Next 1374 0 R
>>
endobj
1363 0 obj
<<
/Title (Social factors play a crucial role in shaping youth mental health outcomes. Family relationships, peer interactions, socioeconomic conditions, exposure to violence, and discrimination can either protect or harm well-being. The expansion of digital platforms has created both opportunities and risks. While social media can raise awareness and build supportive communities, it can also contribute to cyberbullying, social comparison, and excessive screen use, increasing risks of anxiety and depression.)
/Dest [16 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1355 0 R
/Prev 1374 0 R
>>
endobj
1364 0 obj
<<
/Title (Poverty, inequality, displacement, and limited education further place vulnerable groups, including refugees and youth in conflict-affected areas, at higher risk.)
/Dest [17 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1356 0 R
/Next 1375 0 R
>>
endobj
1365 0 obj
<<
/Title (Institutional factors remain central to improving youth mental health worldwide. Many countries face shortages of trained professionals, limited funding, and weak policy frameworks. Schools often lack sufficient counseling services and mental health education. International efforts led by WHO, UNICEF, and UNESCO, alongside Sustainable Development Goal 3: Good Health and Well-being, aim to strengthen systems, expand access to care, reduce stigma, and integrate mental health support into education and healthcare.)
/Dest [17 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1356 0 R
/Prev 1375 0 R
>>
endobj
